## Highlights
Simulation study of perovskite solar cells with $p{-}i{-}n$ structure in the configuration $\mathrm{Cu}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{O}_{x}/\mathrm{MAPb}\mathrm{X}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}$ . • Solar cells with $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{2}\mathbf{Br}$ photoactive layers exhibit best conversion efficiency and show promise of high thermal stability. Simulation results provide a better understanding of the defect density and thickness of the active perovskite layer.
Abstract Although perovskite solar cells with power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) more than $22\%$ have been realized with expensive organic charge-transporting materials, their stability and high cost remain to be addressed. In this work, the perovskite configuration of MAPbX $(\mathbf{MA}=\mathbf{CH}_{3}\mathbf{NH}_{3}$ , ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}}={\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{r}}_{3}$ , or $\mathbf{I}_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}$ ) integrated with stable and low-cost $\mathrm{Cu:NiO}_{x}$ hole-transporting material, $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ electron-transporting material, and Al counter electrode was modeled as a planar PSC and studied theoretically. A solar cell simulation program (wxAMPS), which served as an update of the popular solar cell simulation tool (AMPS: Analysis of Microelectronic and Photonic Structures), was used. The study yielded a detailed understanding of the role of each component in the solar cell and its effect on the photovoltaic parameters as a whole. The bandgap of active materials and operating temperature of the modeled solar cell were shown to influence the solar cell performance in a significant way. Further, the simulation results reveal a strong dependence of photovoltaic parameters on the thickness and defect density of the light-absorbing layers. Under moderate simulation conditions, the $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{2}\mathbf{Br}$ cells recorded the highest PCEs of 20.58 and $19.08\%$ , respectively, while $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ cell gave a value of $16.14\%$ .
